Angular Signals
What are Signals?

Signals are a new reactive state management feature introduced in Angular. They allow Angular to automatically track state changes and update the UI whenever the signal value changes.

Before Signals, Angular mainly relied on:

    Change Detection

    RxJS Observables

    @Input() and @Output()

Signals provide a simpler and more efficient way to manage state.
Why Angular Introduced Signals?

Traditional Angular change detection checks many components even if only one value changes.

Signals solve this problem by:

    Tracking exactly which data changes

    Updating only the affected UI

    Improving performance

    Writing less code

    Eliminating many manual subscriptions

Creating a Signal

import { signal } from '@angular/core';

count = signal(0);

Read:

console.log(count());

Update:

count.set(10);

count.update(value => value + 1);

Counter Example

import { Component, signal } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-counter',
  standalone: true,
  template: `
    <h2>{{ count() }}</h2>
    <button (click)="increment()">+</button>
    <button (click)="decrement()">-</button>
  `
})
export class CounterComponent {
  count = signal(0);

  increment() {
    this.count.update(v => v + 1);
  }

  decrement() {
    this.count.update(v => v - 1);
  }
}

Computed Signals

import { signal, computed } from '@angular/core';

price = signal(500);
quantity = signal(2);

total = computed(() => price() * quantity());

Effects

import { signal, effect } from '@angular/core';

count = signal(0);

effect(() => {
  console.log(count());
});

Objects

user = signal({
  name: 'Karan',
  age: 22
});

user.update(current => ({
  ...current,
  age: 23
}));

Arrays

users = signal(['Karan', 'Rahul']);

users.update(list => [...list, 'Aman']);

Signal vs Observable
Signal	Observable
Holds current value	Emits stream of values
No subscription	Requires subscription or async pipe
Synchronous	Often asynchronous
Great for component state	Great for HTTP/WebSockets
Best Practices

    Use signal() for component state.

    Use computed() for derived values.

    Use effect() for side effects only.

    Update arrays/objects immutably.

    Use Observables for HTTP and async streams.

Summary

Angular Signals provide fine-grained reactivity with automatic UI updates, improved performance, and cleaner code. They are ideal for managing local component state, while RxJS Observables remain the preferred solution for asynchronous operations such as HTTP requests and WebSockets.
